# Building Custom Make.com Apps

##### The HTTP module that became forty HTTP modules

An agency integrated a client's regional logistics API into Make using the generic HTTP module. It worked. Then they built a second scenario for the same client, and a third, and eventually eleven.

Every one of those scenarios repeated the same base URL, the same authentication header construction, the same error interpretation. When the vendor moved from `/v2/` to `/v3/` and changed its auth header name, the agency edited forty-odd modules across eleven scenarios. Three were missed. Those three broke silently and were discovered by the client.

A **custom app** would have made this one change in one place. That is the core argument: custom apps turn a repeated integration pattern into a reusable, versioned component with proper connections, typed fields, and a single point of maintenance.

##### App structure

A Make custom app is defined in JSON across a few sections, edited in the browser-based **Custom Apps builder** (Make → Custom apps → Create).

- **Base** — shared configuration inherited by every module: base URL, default headers, global error handling, and response parsing. This is the piece that would have saved the agency.
- **Connections** — how users authenticate. One app can offer several (API key and OAuth2).
- **Modules** — the operations users drag onto a canvas: actions, searches, triggers, plus universal and responder types.
- **RPCs** — Remote Procedure Calls that dynamically populate fields.
- **Webhooks** — for instant triggers.
- **Functions** — reusable IML helper functions available across the app.

The **Base** deserves attention because everything else inherits it:

```json
{
  "baseUrl": "https://api.logistics-vendor.com/v3",
  "headers": {
    "Authorization": "Bearer {{connection.accessToken}}",
    "Accept": "application/json"
  },
  "response": {
    "error": {
      "message": "[{{statusCode}}] {{body.error.message}}",
      "type": "{{if(statusCode = 429, 'RateLimitError', 'RuntimeError')}}"
    }
  },
  "log": {
    "sanitize": [
      "request.headers.authorization",
      "response.body.access_token"
    ]
  }
}
```

Two details worth copying. The `error` block turns raw API responses into readable messages users can act on — "[422] SKU must be alphanumeric" instead of a JSON dump. And `log.sanitize` strips credentials from execution logs, which is a genuine security requirement: without it, every user's bearer token is visible in their scenario history.

Returning a `type` of `RateLimitError` also matters functionally — Make treats certain error types as retriable, so classification here changes runtime behaviour.

##### Connections

**API-key connections** are the simplest. You define the fields users fill in and a validation request:

```json
{
  "name": "logistics-api-key",
  "type": "basic",
  "parameters": [
    {
      "name": "apiKey",
      "type": "text",
      "label": "API Key",
      "required": true,
      "help": "Find this under Settings → Developers → API Keys."
    },
    {
      "name": "region",
      "type": "select",
      "label": "Region",
      "required": true,
      "options": [
        { "label": "India", "value": "in" },
        { "label": "Singapore", "value": "sg" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "communication": {
    "url": "https://api.logistics-vendor.com/v3/me",
    "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer {{parameters.apiKey}}" },
    "response": {
      "metadata": { "value": "{{body.account.email}}", "type": "email" }
    }
  }
}
```

That `metadata.value` becomes the connection's display label — so a user with four accounts sees "ops@acme.in" rather than "Connection 3". Small, and it materially improves the experience.

**OAuth2 connections** require the full four-step definition: `preauthorize` (optional state generation), `authorize` (the consent URL with scopes and redirect), `token` (exchange the code), and `refresh` (renew before expiry). Make supplies the redirect URI you register with the provider, and handles refresh automatically when you set the token's expiry from the response.

The security rule: mark sensitive parameters appropriately and always add them to `log.sanitize`. Users trust apps with their credentials, and execution logs are visible in their organization.

##### Modules

**Action modules** perform an operation. The definition maps user inputs to a request and the response to output fields:

```json
{
  "name": "createShipment",
  "label": "Create a Shipment",
  "description": "Creates a new shipment for an order.",
  "connection": "logistics-api-key",
  "communication": {
    "url": "/shipments",
    "method": "POST",
    "body": {
      "order_ref": "{{parameters.orderRef}}",
      "warehouse_id": "{{parameters.warehouseId}}",
      "service_level": "{{parameters.serviceLevel}}",
      "packages": "{{parameters.packages}}"
    },
    "response": { "output": "{{body.shipment}}" }
  },
  "parameters": [
    { "name": "orderRef", "type": "text", "label": "Order Reference", "required": true },
    {
      "name": "warehouseId", "type": "select", "label": "Warehouse", "required": true,
      "options": { "store": "rpc://listWarehouses" }
    },
    {
      "name": "serviceLevel", "type": "select", "label": "Service Level", "required": true,
      "options": [
        { "label": "Standard (3-5 days)", "value": "std" },
        { "label": "Express (1-2 days)", "value": "exp" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "packages", "type": "array", "label": "Packages",
      "spec": [
        { "name": "weight_kg", "type": "number", "label": "Weight (kg)", "required": true },
        { "name": "sku", "type": "text", "label": "SKU" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "interface": [
    { "name": "id", "type": "text", "label": "Shipment ID" },
    { "name": "tracking_number", "type": "text", "label": "Tracking Number" },
    { "name": "estimated_delivery", "type": "date", "label": "Estimated Delivery" }
  ]
}
```

The `interface` section is what populates the field picker in downstream modules. Omit it and users see raw, unlabelled JSON — one of the clearest markers of an unfinished app.

**Search modules** are structurally similar but return zero or more bundles and never modify data. Providing both a search and a create is what lets users build reliable find-or-create logic.

**Trigger modules** come in two forms, which is the comparison the assignment asks for:

**Polling triggers** — Make calls your endpoint on the scenario's schedule. You must handle three things the app developer owns: **ordering** (return newest first, or configure `order: "asc"` appropriately), **deduplication** via a designated unique field, and **the epoch/checkpoint** — Make stores the last-seen value so the next run resumes from it.

```json
{
  "name": "watchShipments",
  "label": "Watch Shipments",
  "type": "trigger",
  "communication": {
    "url": "/shipments",
    "qs": {
      "created_after": "{{parameters.epoch}}",
      "limit": "{{parameters.limit}}",
      "order": "desc"
    },
    "response": {
      "output": "{{item}}",
      "iterate": "{{body.results}}",
      "trigger": {
        "id": "{{item.id}}",
        "date": "{{item.created_at}}",
        "type": "date",
        "order": "desc"
      }
    }
  },
  "epoch": {
    "communication": { "url": "/shipments", "qs": { "limit": 10, "order": "desc" } },
    "response": { "iterate": "{{body.results}}",
                  "output": { "label": "{{item.tracking_number}}", "date": "{{item.created_at}}" } }
  }
}
```

That `trigger` block is the dedup contract: `id` identifies the record, `date` orders it, and Make uses both to decide what is new. The separate `epoch` section powers the "choose where to start" dialogue users see when first configuring the trigger.

**Instant triggers (webhooks)** require the provider's API to support subscription management. You define `attach` (called when a user activates the scenario — register Make's generated webhook URL with the vendor), `detach` (deregister on deactivation), and the webhook module itself (parse and validate the incoming payload, ideally verifying an HMAC signature).

From the developer's perspective the trade-off is clear: **polling always works and self-heals after outages but is delayed and consumes operations on empty checks; instant is near-real-time and efficient but requires vendor webhook support, correct attach/detach lifecycle handling, and payload verification** — and a delivery lost during downtime is gone unless the vendor retries.

##### RPCs: dynamic fields

An **RPC (Remote Procedure Call)** is a small definition that fetches data at configuration time, not run time. Its job is to populate dropdowns and field lists from the user's own account.

```json
{
  "name": "listWarehouses",
  "communication": {
    "url": "/warehouses",
    "method": "GET",
    "response": {
      "iterate": "{{body.results}}",
      "output": { "label": "{{item.name}} ({{item.city}})", "value": "{{item.id}}" }
    }
  }
}
```

Referenced as `"options": { "store": "rpc://listWarehouses" }`, this turns a free-text warehouse ID field into a friendly picker.

Three RPC types are worth knowing: **Dynamic options** (dropdowns, as above), **Dynamic fields** (return an entire field *specification* — essential when the schema depends on user choice, such as a CRM's custom fields per object type), and **Dynamic samples** (produce sample output so downstream mapping works before any real run).

RPCs are why a custom app feels native. Without them users type opaque IDs; with them they select "Pune Warehouse (Pune)".

##### Testing, versioning, publishing

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Define base<br/>+ connection] --> B[Build module<br/>in editor]
    B --> C[Run module<br/>inline test]
    C --> D[Use in a real<br/>scenario]
    D --> E{Works?}
    E -->|no| F[Check request/response<br/>in the debug panel]
    F --> B
    E -->|yes| G[Create new<br/>app version]
    G --> H{Share scope}
    H -->|private| I[Invite by URL<br/>no review]
    H -->|public| J[Submit for<br/>Make review]
```

The builder's inline testing shows the exact request sent and response received, which is where nearly all debugging happens — a mismatched header or a body Make serialized differently than you expected is immediately visible.

**Versioning** follows the same compatibility discipline as any published API: existing scenarios are bound to the version they were built against. Adding parameters and interface fields is safe; renaming or removing them breaks live scenarios belonging to other people. When a breaking change is genuinely necessary, create a new version and leave the old one available.

**Publishing** is a choice between private (share via invitation link, no review, ideal for internal or single-client apps) and public in Make's app directory (review process covering auth, error messages, labels, help text and dedup correctness).

##### Common pitfalls

- **No `interface` section**, leaving users with raw unlabelled output.
- **Credentials not sanitized** in logs, exposing tokens in execution history.
- **Missing or unstable trigger `id`**, causing repeated or missed triggers.
- **Wrong sort order** on a polling trigger, so the checkpoint advances incorrectly and records are skipped.
- **Forgetting `detach`** on instant triggers, leaking webhook subscriptions at the vendor.
- **Hard-coded base URLs in modules** instead of the Base — the original problem this solves.
- **Unhelpful error messages** that pass raw API JSON to the user.
- **No pagination handling** in search modules, silently returning only the first page.
- **Breaking changes without a new version.**

##### Your hands-on tie-in

The practical task: in the **custom app builder**, create an app for a public API — configure the **base and an API-key connection**, then build **one action and one search module**, and test them in a real scenario. Pay attention to the `interface` definition; run the module, then check that the field picker in the next module shows friendly labels rather than raw JSON. That difference is the whole point of building an app instead of using HTTP modules.

The mini project is a fuller app: **OAuth2 connection**, a **polling trigger with deduplication**, an **action module**, and at least one **RPC-powered dynamic dropdown**, versioned and tested against real API calls. Test the dedup contract explicitly — run the trigger, add one record at the source, run again, and confirm exactly one new bundle appears.

For the assignment, explain **what an RPC is used for** and compare **polling versus instant triggers from the app developer's perspective**, including the deduplication and registration concerns of each. The developer-side framing is the important part: polling means you own ordering, dedup and the epoch; instant means you own the attach/detach lifecycle and payload verification. Those are different obligations, not merely different latencies.

##### Key takeaways

- A custom app centralizes base URL, auth, error interpretation and field definitions so one vendor change is one edit rather than forty.
- Define the Base carefully — inherited headers, readable error messages with types, and log sanitization for credentials.
- Modules need an `interface` for friendly downstream mapping, and pairing a search with a create enables reliable find-or-create for users.
- Polling triggers put ordering, deduplication and checkpoint handling on you; instant triggers require attach/detach lifecycle management and payload verification.
- RPCs make apps feel native by populating dropdowns, field specs and samples from the user's own account — and version discipline protects scenarios you cannot see.
